WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.800
Baron Jay, actor/producer: We met about 6
years ago and we kind of like reconnected

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:08.120
through our mutual Detroit Farmer maybe like
three or four years ago.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.460
So it’s been a great ride.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.410
Michael Joseph, actor/producer: Great ride.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.360
He’s calling us to set.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.630
Joe Bohn, first AD: I do have to hold for
these guys.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.240
I love your BTS but I’ve got a picture to
make.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.240
Alright, let’s go guys.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.420
Michael Joseph: Thank you guys.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.980
Joe Bohn: My name is Joe Bohn.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.050
I’m the first assistant director on DEADLY
REUNION.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.050
[Joe on set directing actors and crew]

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.050
“She’ll be on your right.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.050
Monica will be on your left.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.050
So Monica let’s actually start you here.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.050
We’ll actually start you ahead.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.210
I know that’s not how you actually did it
before but we’ll start you ahead just for

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.930
safety sake in doing this.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.930
So real hand speed here’s the action.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.840
We walk in…you’re with them, you’re
with them.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.040
And then Chuckie will come into here.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.390
So let’s just pick it up from right there
guys.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.190
We get clustery in that top end.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.770
So Chuckie this is your one, Stu let’s have
you Chuckie’s on the right.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.620
Jill let’s have you on the left (this is
where you’re going to end up).

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.020
I want to clean this up a little bit.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.810
Stu you are on the right of the camera…there
we go.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.520
So we start going through the stuff like you
guys do, so let’s start going through the

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.520
stuff.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.470
Film Courage: How did you get attached to
the project, Joe?

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.290
Joe Bahn: Joe Karam who is one of the actors
(he plays Bobby) he was in negotiations to

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.870
come onto the project and we worked together
once before and he wanted to make sure that

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:20.970
if he was going to be a part of it he wanted
it to be ran really well from an on set perspective

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.930
and made one of his conditions that I be hired
on as well before he would accept his involvement

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:27.780
so that’s how I got on.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:28.780
[Joe on set]

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.780
“Here we go!

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:30.780
Quiet all around please.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:31.780
Picture’s up.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.780
Slated and roll sound.”

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:33.780
“Sound speed”

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.780
“Call it”

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.610
“18 Apple - Take 2”

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:37.610
“Camera”

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.610
“Mark”

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:39.610
“Let me know”

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.010
Film Courage: And how long have you been first
ADing?

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.680
Joe Bohn: Professionally first AD…a little
over 5 years?

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.240
I moved out from San Francisco (I got my Master’s
up there) and one of my associates moved down

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.700
here as well booked a first AD job for Miley
Cyrus for one of her videos.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.729
He brought me on as a second AD.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:01.780
The producers liked him (but they didn’t
want to bring him back) so they brought me

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.080
back instead and they started bringing me
back over and over again, recommending me

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.379
to other producers and that’s kind of the
history of everything, that’s the history

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:11.379
of my career.

00:02:11.379 --> 00:02:14.790
I just got recommended and just kind of spider-webbed
out from there.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.200
Film Courage: And as a first AD what is your…from
the minute you get on set, what is your responsibility?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.560
What are you looking for at all time?

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.480
Joe Bohn: No dead time.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.099
I mean fundamentally you are trying to get
the greatest amount of quality footage in

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:31.849
the can for the director and the production
at any given time.

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:35.650
And what usually happens on a lot of sets
(especially if you don’t have a strong first)

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.060
is that downtime where people are ready to
work but no one is telling them to work, no

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.900
one is telling them to go there and do that.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.709
It almost in some ways feels like babysitting…I
don’t want to put down other departments,

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:46.889
it’s not like that.

00:02:46.889 --> 00:02:50.980
But people are waiting to do their jobs you
don’t have a foreman (like on a construction

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.450
site) waiting to tell them “Okay guys, you
need to go in and do this.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.540
You need to come out so they can go in and
do this,” then no one knows what to do and

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.470
it’s a lot of people milling around.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:04.309
So for me as soon as we’re in, as soon as
that clock hits 7 o’clock, 8’ o’clock

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:08.430
(whatever time your call time is) it is immediately
getting every department working on something.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:12.400
An ideal schedule or an ideal world or an
ideal world has every department constantly

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.230
working on something whether it’s the scene
that we’re shooting currently, prepping

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.480
the next scene, wrapping out the previous
scene, any elements like that, that we can

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:20.480
do.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.090
We have a B camera that’s not shooting because
of the set-up in this particular scene, then

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.989
let’s get the B camera to go outside and
get B roll.

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:29.730
Let’s get the house, let’s get the cars,
let’s get the sunset, let’s get something.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.439
But if people are sitting around doing nothing,
then that becomes an issue that should be

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:35.209
rectified in an ideal world.

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:36.519
Watch your ears real quick [Joe prepares crew
and actors for upcoming scene] “Nine minutes

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:37.519
guys!

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:38.519
Nine minute warning.”

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:43.389
[Joe back on set preparing crew and actors]

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.510
“Here we go!”

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:50.459
“Are we coming in on this one?”

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:54.689
“Yeah, I’ll try to call it.”

00:03:54.689 --> 00:03:58.230
“So this isn’t afterward…this is…”

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.640
“Pictures up.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.719
Quiet all around.

00:04:00.719 --> 00:04:02.730
“From the top, let’s continue to do it.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.629
We’re going to try some new semantics.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:05.629
Slated!

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:06.629
Roll sound.”

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:07.629
“Rolling.

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:08.629
Call it.”

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:09.629
“Sound speed.”

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:10.629
“Scene 21 Apple - Take 1.”

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:11.629
“Camera.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:12.629
Camera.”

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:13.629
“Speed.”

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:14.629
“Mark it.”

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:15.629
Film Courage: So as a first AD you’re almost
never resting while on set?

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:18.530
It sounds like you’re always looking for
the next step and doing two things at once

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.530
it sounds like?

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:23.870
Joe Bohn: Yeah, as a first AD if you’re
resting, I’m not sure you are working?

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.229
I don’t know how that works?

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:30.199
Look on your bigger budget stuff where you
have a second AD, a second second, multiple

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:31.310
second seconds, key PA, PA.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:35.400
When you have all these personnel and all
these resources at your disposal, sure you

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.979
can step off set and relax.

00:04:36.979 --> 00:04:39.400
If you smoke, have a cigarette, whatever the
case may be.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.970
But if you are working in the let’s say
under $250,000 budget range, you just don’t

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:49.120
have the personnel underneath you to support
you in that kind of way and in order to keep

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.479
the film moving at a pace that usually (again
at those budget levels) it needs to move,

00:04:54.479 --> 00:04:56.979
you have to be “on” at all times.

00:04:56.979 --> 00:05:00.800
You don’t step off set to 10:1 to use the
restroom.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.520
You step off set to 10:1 to grab a bottle
of water and come back and theoretically if

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.789
you’ve got a PA, you radio them and they
get you the water.

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:11.120
Because every second that you step off is
a second that someone may not be working or

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.000
things may not be moving forward.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.629
Those seconds add up over the course of a
day and certainly over the course of a long

00:05:15.629 --> 00:05:16.930
shoot.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:21.530
And what they add up to is shots you couldn’t
get, set-ups you couldn’t grab, performances

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.620
that couldn’t be tweaked because you didn’t
have time and that time gets lost…yes because

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.060
other people weren’t doing their jobs but
fundamentally you weren’t there to supervise

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.500
them and have them continue to work through
that.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.800
[Joe back on set preparing crew and actors]

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.389
“Let me emphasize that, these are your marks,
right?

00:05:36.389 --> 00:05:37.540
These are your marks.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:40.699
Let’s not wander, let’s not waddle, let’s
stay where we are.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:43.169
Harry is the only one who is going to pin
it to the stove to where he is.

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:44.169
Camera we’ve got sharps.”

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:47.289
Film Courage: Challenges for a first AD on
set and how you work around it?

00:05:47.289 --> 00:05:52.320
Joe Bohn: Challenges for a first AD on set
and how you work around it?

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.300
Well, that’s the challenge in and of itself.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:01.139
Each film set inherently comes with its own
variables and its own issues.

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:04.479
It would be very easy to be like “Oh, well
the problems are X, Y and Z and this is how

00:06:04.479 --> 00:06:05.479
you solve them.

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:07.479
Unfortunately that’s not the situation.

00:06:07.479 --> 00:06:10.419
Certainly in this industry but specifically
as a first AD.

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:14.840
You have to first and foremost evaluate what
your resources are and resources refer to

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.250
not only just what you psychically have but
your personnel as well.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.300
Do I have five people or do I have one?

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.970
That’s going to change the problems you
are going encounter and how you can solve

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:24.970
them.

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.539
Ideally you are solving those problems before
you get to them anyways but maybe you’re

00:06:28.539 --> 00:06:29.539
not.

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:32.849
And then obviously the psychical resources
of your equipment, money, the house, what

00:06:32.849 --> 00:06:36.969
time can we shoot, fighting the daylight (if
you’re outside), that kind of stuff.

00:06:36.969 --> 00:06:42.940
So in terms of common problems…I think really
the most common problem you are always going

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:46.020
to run into is personality.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.940
You have people with egos or not with egos,
people that are hard to work with or easy

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:52.419
to work with, but they are green, they are
not experienced, they want to learn.

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:55.540
So you have to teach on set as you go along.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:07:00.360
It’s really…fundamentally it’s not about
being the first AD that you think you should

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.360
be.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.270
It’s about being the first AD that this
particular production, these particular people,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.270
need you to be.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:11.289
I think that’s the problem that a lot of
people who take this position is that they

00:07:11.289 --> 00:07:15.129
come in…the know the rules, they know the
regulations, they know how to do the job,

00:07:15.129 --> 00:07:16.599
but they approach every job the same.

00:07:16.599 --> 00:07:21.479
And in doing so, you can’t fit a square
peg in a round hole.

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:25.210
You have to be malleable yourself and take
those first few days to figure out what this

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:26.560
needs and how to work with people.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.860
And if you can do that and adapt quickly and
efficiently, then hopefully you minimize those

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.389
problems that you are talking about.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:36.850
Film Courage: For you…when you first step
on [set]…let’s suppose you are going to

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:43.510
have a 17-day shoot at what point do you find…that
you get this gut-level “Okay…I think this

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:45.310
is where the issues are going to be.”

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.280
Not specific people (maybe, maybe not) but
where do you get the temperament of the set?

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.999
When does it start to reveal itself to you?

00:07:50.999 --> 00:07:52.240
Joe Bohn: I think by Day 2.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.169
I think if you are really paying attention
probably really (honestly) by lunch on Day

00:07:56.169 --> 00:07:57.169
1.

00:07:57.169 --> 00:08:00.300
Because it’s very easy to see “Okay, he’s
got a chip on his shoulder, he doesn’t communicate

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.910
well, and she’s…”

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:04.979
You can really kind of get that pretty quickly.

00:08:04.979 --> 00:08:07.960
But if haven’t figured out by Day 2, then
you’re not going to figure it out (you’re

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:08.960
just not).

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.699
At that point it should have revealed itself.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:15.139
Now in a 17-day shoot, if you’re moving
from a location-to-location maybe new problems

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:19.840
arise in that sense where you are in this
location but this location is super strict.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.909
They want padding down on the floor, they
want layout board, ram board, and they want

00:08:22.909 --> 00:08:24.190
this, that and the other.

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:29.759
So you have to discover those problems and
then change and acclimate to them extremely

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:33.631
fast because you don’t have the two days
previously to kind of build up to it and work

00:08:33.631 --> 00:08:35.690
out a scheme or a plan to make it work.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.440
So that’s the biggest thing.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.610
Otherwise yeah…if you are paying attention
and you’re on set and you are working with

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.350
people and you are listening and communicating,
I think you find out by the end of the first

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:44.350
day, really.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.520
No later than the end of the second.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.840
Film Courage: I get the sense that you are
really good at reading people.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.830
That you just have this innate thing where
you can figure what someone needs, where they

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.060
need to be lead, am I right with that?

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:56.360
You have a really quick sense with that?

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:57.360
Joe Bohn: Yeah, I think I do.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.050
I think I have a really quick sense to read
people.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:03.460
It makes me as valuable and as good at the
job as I am (I hope it does).

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:08.520
I think without that it becomes much more
difficult to do the job effectively as I want

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.250
to and I strive to do it.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.620
[Joe back on set preparing crew and actors]

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:14.620
“Okay, let’s try one guys.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:15.620
Obviously Chuck has total control here to
say Stop, Cut…whatever the case may be.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:16.620
Let’s go to one.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:17.620
Watch the lights, watch the reflections.”

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.060
“Are we going from one or are we going to…sorry
to cut you off.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:22.650
Are we starting from one?”

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:23.650
“What?

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:24.650
Just starting from right here.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:29.020
Guys, let me have you clear over from this
side just to be safe, then we don’t swing

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.020
and all over the world.”

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:31.020
“Does that include me?”

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:32.020
“No you are okay, Justin.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.020
Thank you.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:34.020
Okay, let’s try it.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:35.020
Cameras going up, pictures going up.”

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.300
Film Courage: So we are on the set of DEADLY
REUNION and this is day eight of….?

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.720
Joe Bohn: Eight of eight is today for DEADLY
REUNION.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:40.880
Film Courage: Eight of eight, okay.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.710
And you have been in this one location.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.760
What have been some of the challenges at this
location and how have you been working through

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:47.760
it?

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:48.760
And also the script changes?

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:49.760
Joe Bohn: Location is simple enough.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.150
I mean it’s a dual coin, right?

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.440
You get challenges and benefits from being
in one location.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.960
The benefits being no location moves, you
don’t have to wrap out which takes time

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.490
every time you do it, you’re taking time
away from shooting, time away from shooting.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.150
Because we are living here, all the gear is
constant.

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:07.210
It’s very easy to come in at the top of
the day, have a 7:00 a.m. call, be shooting

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:08.600
by 8:00 because everything is here.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:14.420
And if you end the previous day correctly
then you can already be lit, art decked and

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:16.740
pre-blocked for the following day.

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.040
So you come and you get right to work.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.130
So that’s the benefits.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:21.720
The problem is you are in one location for
a long time.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:27.100
From an aesthetic point (directorially speaking),
aesthetically you don’t have a lot of variety

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:30.150
in your visual aesthetic, which can bore the
audience.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:33.780
That’s the director in me talking but you
can get the idea.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:36.670
From a practical standpoint as an AD, we are
really on top of each other.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.720
There is gear here, you don’t have a lot
of room to maneuver (this isn’t a mansion,

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.770
it’s a house) to it’s very congested.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:46.990
And it’s a matter of efficiently having
the departments work around each other.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:51.160
So having grip and electric come in, having
them come out and then having art go in.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.580
If everyone just tries to cluster and do their
jobs at the same time, you have a mess and

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:56.580
everything goes slower.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.270
The way to do it efficiently is to make sure
that certain departments have the floor at

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:02.390
certain times to make it work in the quickest
way possible.

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:07.770
Now as for scheduling and the script changes,
so there is a Day 1 rewrite which is fine

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:10.630
if it’s a partial rewrite or a scene rewrite.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:19.120
This is a full rewrite of the script during…that
was decided to be done on Day 1 of photography

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:20.750
(on day one of shooting).

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:25.990
So we shut down a half-day early, the rewrites
started.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:32.840
So as an AD what becomes extremely problematic
and extremely challenging is how do I schedule

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:33.840
the rest of the shoot?

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.380
How do you schedule a film that you don’t
know what is about to take place?

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:38.380
Theoretically I would have a break down.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:41.120
I would know everything that goes on in every
scene, I know what props I need, what wardrobe

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.510
changes, what special effects and I have everything
blocked out and certain ways to shoot it…again,

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:46.510
efficiently.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.620
In this situation I didn’t have that.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:55.290
So the beginning of production was challenging
and stressful I think for a lot of departments,

00:11:55.290 --> 00:12:00.960
especially camera, grip and electric, in that
usually you’d shoot out all the scenes in

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.340
the living room and then all the scenes in
the bathroom and then you’d just be done

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.470
with that room and you’d move on.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:11.140
Because we were getting pages the morning
of, we were pretty much shooting in chronological

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:14.720
order, just living room to bathroom, back
to living room, back to here…because I never

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:15.930
knew what was coming next.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.480
I didn’t have time, the director didn’t
have time, no one really had time to break

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.080
things down and look at it, we just had to
grab them and go.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:27.890
What the challenge there becomes is the morale,
that’s really the challenge.

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:30.580
Because no one really wants to go back…“We're
going back to the living room, again?

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:34.250
For the same lighting setting up that we just
had…Okay?”

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:36.870
And you can just feel that morale drown.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:39.701
And once that starts to go down, you feel
the energy dip.

00:12:39.701 --> 00:12:41.060
When the energy dips, people move slower.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:44.030
Even if people are trying to do their jobs,
they just don’t have the passion for it.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.510
So it’s really the passion of checking my
time, sorry [checks phone for when to be back

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:48.510
on set].

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:49.510
“Five minutes guys!

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:50.510
Five minute warning.”

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:51.510
[Addresses actors and crew]

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.200
So the passion dips and everything moves slower.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:56.250
You don’t get as much footage in the can,
you don’t get as much quality out of your

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.250
personnel.

00:12:57.250 --> 00:13:02.600
What was nice is that we had already scheduled
a three-day break in the middle of week 1

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:03.600
and week 2.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:07.980
So during that three-day break, the screenwriter
who is doing the revisions was able to get

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:09.760
a good chunk of pages out.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:14.160
Now that first week what I was doing was going
home, seeing what few pages I had (four or

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.790
five) and trying to plant some kind of game
plan whatever it might be and get something

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:19.790
out.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:24.440
And that worked…to a level it worked…as
much as it could.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:30.190
But now on the last day of that break I got
15-20 pages.

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:34.720
So there I could actually schedule a day or
two out and actually put some things together

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.910
and block shoot some scenes.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:42.820
So that became much more accessible, much
more tackle-able?

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:47.610
Able to be tackled…(there you go)…much
more able to be tackled than it had previously,

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:52.191
which is where we are now at this point on
Day 8, the last three days we’ve been able

00:13:52.191 --> 00:13:57.980
to run a more legitimately planned set (if
you will) than flying by the cuff of our pants

00:13:57.980 --> 00:13:58.980
than we had.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:03.250
Film Courage: And I understand some people,
actors or whomever are not aware of who is

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:05.550
dying, when and they may be surprised?

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:11.550
Joe Bohn: In the original script nobody really
died per se.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.560
Characters may have left the scene for awhile.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.770
For the most part, they all lived.

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:17.790
One character died toward the end but they
are all in it until the end but everyone is

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:19.970
really in the whole thing.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:23.380
During that day one rewrite conversation,
it was decided that some of those characters

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:26.550
would die to up the drama and the tension.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:28.740
In dining so everyone was like “Okay, yeah…we
are okay with that.”

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:32.920
I don’t think anyone thinks they are going
to be the first to die until they are and

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.580
then they are like “Ahhhh…I died on page
15?

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:38.210
I wanted to be in the movie.”

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:42.610
So that’s the situation, actors coming in
and looking over the pages in the morning

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:43.770
and “Holy!

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:44.770
This is what happens today?”

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:45.770
Or “I died today?”

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:46.820
Or whatever it may be.

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:49.300
And we were doing the same thing, as well.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:51.260
Just reading it and going and going “Wow!

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:53.940
This is going to be a fun day.”

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:55.390
So yeah, they didn’t know it was coming.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:59.380
The tough part about that is budgeting the
time and structuring and things like that

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:04.610
is usually I can look at something and say
“Okay this is a massive fight scene.”

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:08.850
And maybe it only says it’s 1/8th of a page
total on the script but I know it’s going

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:12.070
to be 4 hours of shooting to get it.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:18.400
Without having the time to do that pre-production
work and really break down the script, I’m

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:24.960
left with guessing at how much time certain
things should take strictly based on my previous

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:26.140
experience.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:29.440
Which has actually worked out pretty well
but also could have been a catastrophe.

